{
 "id": "GravityC07",
 "slug": "gravity/c07",
 "chapter": "The top of the hour",
 "topic": "What if gravity doubled every hour?",
 "script": [
  {
   "beat": "p1",
   "text": "Nine o'clock. Eight times normal."
  },
  {
   "beat": "p2",
   "text": "In the water, you can breathe. Barely. Your mom, Ellie, Mr. Patel, Biscuit, all floating with just your faces out, staring at the sky."
  },
  {
   "beat": "p3",
   "text": "The whole street is in that pool. Nobody talks. Breathing takes all you've got."
  },
  {
   "beat": "p3b",
   "text": "High above you, a plane you can barely see is still coming down, slow and careful, toward the highway. It makes it. Mr. Patel lets out a breath he's been holding for an hour."
  },
  {
   "beat": "p4",
   "text": "Nine fifty-nine. Ellie squeezes your hand. Sixteen g is coming."
  },
  {
   "beat": "p5",
   "text": "Ten o'clock."
  },
  {
   "beat": "p6",
   "text": "And gravity doesn't double."
  },
  {
   "beat": "p7",
   "text": "It disappears."
  },
  {
   "beat": "p8",
   "text": "The water lifts out of the pool like a giant wobbling bubble. With all of you still inside it. Floating up. Past the roof. Past the trees."
  },
  {
   "beat": "p9",
   "text": "And down the street, the whole ocean is starting to rise."
  },
  {
   "beat": "p10",
   "text": "Is any of this real? The physics is. Your blood really does weigh more as gravity grows. Pilots really do black out at a few g. And water really does push back harder as gravity gets stronger. The sources are in the description."
  },
  {
   "beat": "p11",
   "text": "But who flipped the switch at ten o'clock? Tell me your theory in the comments. Part two is coming."
  }
 ],
